<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · sea</p><h1 class="enc-title">Aral Sea</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Aral Sea is a large inland lake in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a>, between present-day Kazakhstan and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzbekistan">Uzbekistan</a>, fed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxus">Amu Darya</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syr-darya">Syr Darya</a> rivers. The steppe lands around it marked the northern fringes of the Achaemenid world. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oghuz-turks">Oghuz Turks</a> who founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seljuk-empire">Seljuk Empire</a> originated in the region near the lower Syr Darya and the Aral Sea.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/aral-sea.jpg" alt="Aral Sea" width="320" height="156" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: National Aeronautics and Space Administration (NASA), collage by Dylan240. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aral_Sea_1964_vs._August_25,_2026.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
